NAME
====

**wattle-projects** - how Wattle projects, libraries and dependencies are laid out

DESCRIPTION
===========

A project is a directory containing an _info file_, source files (in either
Wattle or Zig) and documentation. `wattle-build(1)` builds the exes and libs
that a project declares, `wattle-deps(1)` manages the dependencies of a project and
`wattle-test(1)` runs its tests. This page describes what a project contains.

A project may include one or more _applications_, installable by
`wattle-apps(1)`. For more information on this, see `wattle-applications(7)`. A
project that other projects use is a _library_. See `<LIBRARIES>`.

LAYOUT
======

A project has an ``info.edn`` at its root. This is the _info file_. Every other
file is optional.

INFO FILE
=========

The info file holds one map. All keys are optional, but ``:name`` and
``:group`` are needed to install a project as an application:

- ``:name``:
  The name of the project. It is one directory name and is unique across
  groups.

- ``:group``:
  The group of the project, by convention a reverse domain such as
  ``org.example``. The value is used in a directory name and so cannot be
  empty, ``.`` or ``..``. It cannot contain a path separator.

- ``:vendored``:
  A vector of maps that describe the sources that ``wattle deps vendor`` copies
  into the project. Each has a ``:name`` and a ``:url``, and may have a
  ``:ref``, a ``:prefix`` and ``:paths``. See `<VENDORING>` and
  `wattle-deps(1)`.

- ``:exes``:
  A vector of maps that describe the programs of the project. Each has a
  ``:type``, ``:native``, ``:source`` or ``:web``, a ``:name`` and an
  ``:entry``. See 'Declarations' in `wattle-build(1)`.

- ``:libs``:
  A vector of maps that describe the libraries of the project. Each has a
  ``:type``, ``:native``, ``:source`` or ``:remote``, a ``:name`` and may have
  ``:for``, the names of the exes it is part of. See `<LIBRARIES>`.

- ``:docs``:
  A vector of maps that describe the documentation of the project. Each has a
  ``:type``, ``:man`` or ``:web``, ``:files`` and may have ``:for``, the names
  of the exes it is part of. A ``:man`` doc's section is the digit that ends
  each file name, so ``man/greet.1`` is in section 1.

Example:

````
{:name "greeter"
 :group "org.example"
 :vendored [{:name "greetings"
             :url "https://example.org/greetings.git"
             :prefix "deps/greetings"}]
 :exes [{:type :native
         :name "hello"
         :entry "main.wattle"}]
 :libs [{:type :source
         :name "util"
         :files ["util/"]
         :for ["hello"]}]
 :docs [{:type :man
         :files ["man/hello.1"]
         :for ["hello"]}]}
````

LIBRARIES
=========

A library is code that a project uses and does not run by itself. A project
declares the libraries it imports by name in ``:libs`` in its info file, and
``wattle build lib`` places each on the module path. A bare import, as in
``(import greet)``, finds a library by its ``:name``. See 'MODULES' in
`wattle(7)`.

There are three kinds of declaration:

- ``:source``:
  Wattle source files in the project. ``wattle build lib`` copies its
  ``:files`` to ``.wattle/<name>/``, where each keeps its path.

- ``:native``:
  A native module, built from its Zig ``:root`` file in ``zig-out/lib``. A
  native executable whose name the ``:for`` of the library holds links it.

- ``:remote``:
  A library in another project, fetched from ``:url`` at a pinned ``:ref`` or
  ``:hash``. Its own info file declares it as ``:source`` or ``:native``, and
  it is placed as that form.

``.wattle`` holds material that is not committed. ``wattle build lib`` creates
it with a ``.gitignore`` that excludes its contents.

``wattle apps install`` installs a library only as part of an exe whose name
its ``:for`` holds. A library with no ``:for`` is not installed. See
`wattle-applications(7)`.

VENDORING
=========

Vendoring a dependency refers to the practice of copying into the source tree of
a project the Wattle source files that the project uses. A vendored dependency
is not on the module path. Within the code, it is imported using a relative
import:

````
(import ./deps/greetings)
````

A vendored copy is the version the project was tested with, it changes only
when the project's author changes it and the project runs without any
dependency being fetched. A project includes a copy of each dependency and a
fix to a dependency reaches the project only when its author updates the copy.

A ``:remote`` library is the alternative. The project names the source and pins
a revision of it, and the copy is fetched into ``.wattle`` and is not
committed. ``:for`` does not affect vendoring: any library of another project
may be vendored.

The project lists what it vendors under ``:vendored`` in its info file, and
``wattle deps vendor`` copies each entry into the project. See
`wattle-deps(1)`. An entry without a ``:ref`` uses the HEAD of the default
branch of a Git repository each time. The copies belong to the project and may
be edited, and no record is kept of the revision they came from.

